# Mic Buddy (Powered by TrackBarz) — Cursor Project Rules

You are assisting on a 10-day MVP sprint for Mic Buddy, a web app that detects BPM in uploaded beats, counts bars in real-time with tight audio-synced UI, lets users mark song sections (Intro/Verse/Hook/Bridge/Outro), and provides a sectioned lyric notebook.

The single most important quality bar: the bar counter MUST feel locked to the beat. Audio timing precision beats every other concern.

## Stack (Non-Negotiable)
- Next.js 14+ App Router, TypeScript strict mode
- React Server Components by default; 'use client' only when necessary
- Prisma ORM + PostgreSQL (local via Docker Compose)
- Clerk for authentication (v5+ syntax)
- Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui components
- Zod for all input validation
- React Hook Form for forms
- pnpm as package manager
- Web Audio API for all audio work (no external audio frameworks unless explicitly approved)
- web-audio-beat-detector for BPM detection (approved dependency)

## Critical Audio Conventions (Days 3+)
- NEVER use setInterval, setTimeout, or Date.now() for audio-synced UI updates
- ALWAYS drive audio-synced UI from audioContext.currentTime inside a requestAnimationFrame loop
- Bar position math: barDuration = (60 / bpm) * beatsPerBar; currentBar = Math.floor(currentTime / barDuration)
- Expose a manual downbeat offset (±ms) on every session — auto-detection will be wrong sometimes
- Always include a "Tap Tempo" fallback path; auto-BPM is a hint, not truth
- Audio code lives in /lib/audio/* — never scatter AudioContext logic across components
- Single shared AudioContext per page; lazily instantiated on first user gesture (browser policy)
- Prefer AudioBufferSourceNode for playback during the MVP; migrate to AudioWorklet only if latency demands it

## File Structure
- /app — Next.js pages and route handlers
- /app/api — REST route handlers
- /components — shared UI (prefix shadcn primitives with "ui/")
- /components/audio — bar counter, beat indicator, section timeline
- /lib/db.ts — Prisma singleton
- /lib/auth.ts — Clerk-to-Prisma user bridge (getOrCreateUser)
- /lib/audio — all Web Audio API logic (engine, bpm, bars, playback)
- /lib/utils.ts — cn() helper and small utilities only
- /prisma — schema.prisma, migrations, seed.ts
- /shared — Zod schemas and shared TypeScript types (imported by both client and server)

## API Conventions
- Every route handler validates input with Zod before touching the DB
- Error response shape is ALWAYS:
  { error: { code: string, message: string, details?: unknown } }
- Status codes: 400 for validation, 401 for unauth, 403 for forbidden, 404 for not found, 500 only for true server failures
- Every protected route calls getOrCreateUser() and returns 401 if null
- Session ownership checks: a user can only read/write their own Session rows — verify userId match before any mutation
- Use NextResponse.json() — never raw Response objects

## Data Model Rules
- Prisma User has clerkUserId (unique) — this is the bridge, not Clerk's id directly in other tables
- Every user-owned row has userId referencing User.id (not clerkUserId)
- Cascade deletes: deleting a User cascades to Sessions; deleting a Session cascades to Markers, Notes, LyricBlocks
- Timestamps: createdAt @default(now()), updatedAt @updatedAt on all mutable tables
- Never expose clerkUserId to the client — send only our internal User.id if needed

## Client vs Server Components
- Default to Server Components — they're faster and simpler
- Add 'use client' only when you need: useState, useEffect, event handlers, browser APIs (AudioContext, MediaRecorder), or client-only libraries
- Audio components are always client components
- Pages that fetch data should be server components that pass data to client children

## UI Conventions
- shadcn/ui components live in /components/ui — do not modify them directly; wrap if you need custom behavior
- Use the cn() utility from /lib/utils.ts for conditional classNames
- Mic Buddy palette (from brand mockups):
  - Background: cream (#FAF6E9 or similar warm off-white)
  - Primary: navy (#1E2A5E)
  - Accent: red/orange for recording states (#E74C3C)
- The bar counter display is the signature screen — giant numerals, minimal chrome, always centered
- Mobile-first: test at 375px width before desktop

## State Management
- Use React state (useState, useReducer) for local component state
- Use React Server Components + Server Actions or route handlers for server state — no Redux, no Zustand for Day 1–10
- Audio engine state (playing, currentTime, currentBar, bpm) lives in a single custom hook (useAudioEngine) consumed by components

## Error Handling
- Wrap all route handlers in try/catch; log server errors to console with context
- Client-side, use react-hot-toast or shadcn's Sonner for user-facing errors
- Never swallow errors silently
- AudioContext failures (user denied permission, unsupported browser) must show a clear UI message with next steps

## Performance Non-Negotiables
- The requestAnimationFrame loop driving the bar counter must not allocate objects per frame — reuse references
- No console.log calls in audio hot paths (ship with them stripped)
- Prisma queries should use select/include explicitly — never return entire rows by default when only a few fields are needed
- Image uploads: enforce max 20MB server-side; reject anything else with a clear error

## Security
- Never commit .env, .env.local, or any file with secrets
- Never log Clerk tokens, user emails, or session IDs in production code
- All API inputs go through Zod — no trusting client shapes
- Session ownership must be verified server-side on every mutation (defense in depth, even though middleware protects the route)

## What NOT to Do (10-Day Sprint Discipline)
- Do not write tests unless I explicitly ask — this is an MVP sprint, not TDD
- Do not add dependencies without asking — check package.json first and propose before installing
- Do not refactor working code to be "cleaner" unless I ask
- Do not scaffold features beyond the current day's scope (see Day Plan section)
- Do not use AI/LLM APIs, Whisper, or any transcription/generation service in Days 1–10 — those are Phase 2
- Do not introduce Redux, Zustand, tRPC, or GraphQL — REST route handlers only
- Do not use Server Actions for Day 1–2 (route handlers are more debuggable early on)
- Do not use localStorage or sessionStorage (not supported in some deployment contexts) — use React state

## 10-Day Scope (For Your Awareness)
- Day 1: Scaffold, auth, Clerk-Prisma user bridge, empty dashboard ← current day unless told otherwise
- Day 2: Session CRUD, dashboard list, session detail shell
- Day 3: Audio upload, basic playback via Web Audio API
- Day 4: BPM detection, bar grid math, tap tempo, downbeat offset
- Day 5: Real-time bar counter UI (the signature screen)
- Day 6: Section markers, timeline strip, current-section label
- Day 7: Sectioned lyric notebook with autosave
- Day 8: Loop section, keyboard shortcuts, polish
- Day 9: Design pass, mobile, PWA manifest, brand application
- Day 10: Deploy (Vercel + Neon), end-to-end testing, demo video

If I ask for something from a later day, remind me of the scope and ask if I want to pull it forward.

## Communication Style
- Be direct and concise; this is a fast sprint
- When proposing solutions, lead with the code, then brief rationale
- Call out risks explicitly (e.g., "this will drift by ~20ms per minute because...")
- If a request conflicts with these rules, push back before complying
- If unsure between two approaches, ask one clarifying question rather than guessing

## Project Voice
- Brand is Mic Buddy (front-facing) powered by TrackBarz (engine layer)
- Copy should feel like a helpful studio buddy — warm but not cheesy
- Developer voice in comments: concise, professional, no filler
